+<h2 id="guide-21-animation-when-things-happen">21. Animation: when things happen</h2>
+<blockquote>
+<p>Tour: <code>21-animation.alea</code></p>
+</blockquote><figure class="strip"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="" loading="lazy" width="150" height="150"><figcaption>21-animation.alea, edition 0x2a</figcaption></figure>
+<p><code>t</code> runs from 0 to 1 over a video, and anything can follow it. But writing
+"from 0.2 to 0.35, grow, overshooting a little" by hand is arithmetic. These
+functions say it directly:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><strong><code>progress(start, end)</code></strong>: 0 before <code>start</code>, 1 after <code>end</code>, a straight
+  line between. The building block: <code>progress(0.2, 0.35)</code> is a little clock
+  that runs only during that stretch.</li>
+<li><strong><code>ease(v, kind)</code></strong>: the same journey from 0 to 1, with a feel: <code>"linear"</code>,
+  <code>"in"</code> (starts slowly), <code>"out"</code> (arrives slowly), <code>"in-out"</code> (the default),
+  <code>"sine"</code>, <code>"back"</code> (overshoots, then settles), <code>"elastic"</code> (wobbles) or
+  <code>"bounce"</code> (lands, bounces, lands).</li>
+<li><strong><code>keys([[time, value], …], ease)</code></strong>: a value that passes through key
+  frames. The values can be numbers, fields or points:
+  <code>keys([[0, [0.2, 0.7]], [0.5, [0.5, 0.2]], [1, [0.2, 0.7]]], ease: "in-out")</code>
+  is a point that travels and comes back.</li>
+<li><strong><code>sequence([a, b, c], fade)</code></strong>: one thing after another, each holding an
+  equal share of the time, crossfading over <code>fade</code>. Fields or paintings.</li>
+<li><strong><code>at(work, time)</code></strong>: a field or a painting at another moment.
+  <code>at(Culture(), 0.3)</code> is frozen, <code>at(Culture(), 1 - t)</code> runs backwards, and
+  <code>at(Culture(), ease(t, "out"))</code> slows down to its end. Inside the work,
+  time is whatever you give it, all the way down.</li>
+</ul>
+<pre><code class="language-alea">let pop  = ease(progress(0.2, 0.35), &quot;back&quot;)
+let halo = ring(0.5, 0.5, 0.5 * pop, 0.015, 0.01)
+let ball = circle(0.12, 0.1 + 0.8 * ease(fract(t * 4), &quot;bounce&quot;), 0.05)
+render video(sequence([paint(calm), paint(storm), paint(still)], fade: 0.15), duration: 12)
+</code></pre>
+<p>All of these take <code>time:</code> to follow another clock than <code>t</code>: <code>fract(t * 4)</code>
+repeats four times, <code>progress(…, time: other)</code> follows anything.</p>
+<hr />
+<h2 id="guide-22-filters">22. Filters</h2>
+<blockquote>
+<p>Tour: <code>22-filters.alea</code></p>
+</blockquote><figure class="strip"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="" loading="lazy" width="150" height="150"><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," alt="" loading="lazy" width="150" height="150"><figcaption>22-filters.alea, edition 0x2a</figcaption></figure>
+<p>A filter changes how a picture is seen, not what it is. There are two
+moments to apply one.</p>
+<p><strong>On a field</strong>, before colour, so the result is still a field you can
+compute with:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><code>blur(f, 0.01)</code>: softened over that distance (a fraction of the canvas).</li>
+<li><code>pixelate(f, 0.02)</code>: in square cells of that size.</li>
+<li><code>edges(f)</code>: only where the field changes, the outlines of shapes.</li>
+<li><code>relief(f, angle: 135, depth: 10)</code>: lit from one side (the angle the light
+  comes from, 90 above, 135 the upper left), like an engraving.</li>
+<li><code>posterize(f, 6)</code>: in six flat levels.</li>
+<li><code>film(f, 0.08)</code>: grain, new at every frame.</li>
+<li><code>vignette(f, 0.5)</code>: darker towards the corners.</li>
+</ul>
+<p><strong>On a painting</strong>, after colour, as the very last step: <code>blur</code>, <code>pixelate</code>,
+<code>film</code> and <code>vignette</code> work on its pixels too, and two more only make sense
+in colour:</p>
+<ul>
+<li><code>bloom(p, 0.8)</code>: light bleeding out of the bright parts.</li>
+<li><code>chroma(p, 0.5)</code>: red and blue drifting apart towards the edges, like a
+  cheap lens.</li>
+</ul>
+<pre><code class="language-alea">let night = paint(max(lights, land * 0.3), palette: &quot;ember&quot;)
+render image(vignette(film(chroma(bloom(night, 2.5), 0.8), 0.06), 0.6))
+</code></pre>
+<p>A field blur samples the field 40 times around each point, so it costs 40
+times the field; a painting blur works on pixels and costs the same whatever
+is underneath. For a large blur on a heavy work, paint it first.</p>
